# Kotlin

- **Write for a human reader first.** Names say what the thing is or does, unabbreviated (`recalculateUserProgress`, not `recalc`; `hasPendingEntry`, not `flag`). Read top-down, early `return` over nesting, a named intermediate over a dense chain. Headers and why-comments follow `docs.mdc`, as a `/* … */` block above `package` (ktfmt leaves a block comment alone; it rewraps long `//` lines).
- **Layers are Gradle modules; the build is the gate.** `:domain` (pure types, rules, knobs; the standard library only) ← `:usecases` (use-cases + their ports) ← `:data` (adapters) ← `:app` (the Android app, the composition root). Code can only import a module its build file depends on, so a reach past a layer is a compile error. `LAYERS` in `build.gradle.kts` says who may use whom and which libraries each layer takes; a dependency off its row, a module with no row, or a row naming no module fails every build before a task runs. Widening a row is a decision, not a fix (`docs/ports-and-adapters.md`). `checkLayerResolution` walks every component the classpaths resolve to, so a library added by a component metadata rule or a substitution fails too, and so does a file on a classpath (outside an adapter row) or a compile task whose classpath isn't its `compileClasspath`; what an allowed library brings with it is listed in `LIBRARY_DEPENDENCIES`, and a version bump that adds one fails until it is listed. The JDK is outside Gradle's reach, so two more gates cover it: detekt's `ForbiddenImport`/`ForbiddenMethodCall` (imports and the clock, environment, and `Class.forName` calls), and `checkBytecode`, which scans each module's compiled classes, Kotlin and Java. `:domain` has an allowlist (`PURE_BYTECODE_ALLOWED`: the stdlib's pure packages, `java.lang` value types, `java.util` collections, `java.math`, `java.time`, `UUID`, `Charset`), so I/O, threads, processes, the environment, logging, reflection, the clock, and randomness are refused without anyone listing them; members of allowed classes that read the clock, the default zone, locale, or charset, or are random (`LocalDate.now`, `ZoneId.systemDefault`, `UUID.randomUUID`, every `random*`/`shuffle*` under `kotlin/`, `printStackTrace`) are denied on top. `String.format` stays denied: the scan sees member names, not overloads, so `format(Locale.ROOT, …)` looks like the default-locale one; use a string template. Other rows have a denylist (a use-case gets no I/O, processes, threads or thread pools, timers, logging, preferences, reflection, or by-name loading). Allowing a pure API is a decision, not a fix. The scan sees what an import rule can't: a fully qualified name, a typealias, an inline stdlib function, a class loaded by name. Java in a scanned module fails `check` unless its row sets `allowsJava`. Test fixtures can't enter production code, a source directory must sit inside its module, and a detekt baseline fails the build (a `detekt-baseline*.xml` file, or a baseline set on detekt's extension or a task, under any name). `ignoreFailures` on a test or detekt task (or detekt's extension) and detekt's `failOnSeverity` Never fail the build. `@Suppress("all")` and `@Suppress("warnings")` fail `check` in any spelling (`@[…]`, `@file:[…]`), and so do a name that isn't a plain `"Literal"`, an import alias or typealias of `Suppress`, and a suppression with no `//` reason (an empty `//` isn't one).
- **Every gate is proved.** A path glob that matches nothing is silent, so `pnpm kotlin:gates` plants a violation per gate (layer edges, libraries, fixtures, detekt baselines, every bytecode rule written fully qualified, every forbidden import, suppressions, a skipped-test module, an Android API in each logic layer, every setting that quiets Android lint, and more) in a scratch copy and fails unless the build names each. The gates read the layer map from the build (`./gradlew printLayers`), so renaming a module or package means editing `settings.gradle.kts`, `LAYERS`, and detekt's `includes`; adding a bytecode rule means adding its sample to `stacks/kotlin/gates.mjs`. The gates also hold a floor the rows can't go below: every sample in `BYTECODE_SAMPLES` must be a rule some row still has, the pure row needs an allowlist that refuses every family in `OUTSIDE_ALLOWLIST_SAMPLES` and lets `PURE_CORPUS` (ordinary pure Kotlin: enums under `when`, dates, decimals, ids, Base64) through, and every row but an adapter row (`ANY_LIBRARY`) needs bytecode rules or an allowlist and must set `requiresTests` unless its `noTestsReason` says why (the override). A probe in `check` counts only when its own task fails (`> Task :domain:detektMain FAILED`), so a finding printed by a task that passed proves nothing. Weakening a row means editing the gates too, in the same reviewed diff. Locally the gates skip when nothing they read changed since they last passed (`testing-kotlin.mdc`).
- **What the gates are for:** making drift loud (an agent or contributor crossing a layer, a lint, or a test) and refusing settings that switch a gate off. They are not a sandbox against someone set on evading them: a cheap deny goes in, and the classes they knowingly don't stop are listed in `docs/deferred.md` (Kotlin pack → what the gates don't stop).
- **Why not Konsist:** the module graph already makes the compiler refuse a cross-layer import, and the one hole (the JDK) is a single detekt rule in the same lint run. Konsist would add a second rules engine whose scopes also pass silently when empty. Add it when a project needs package rules *inside* a module.
- **Lint and format are loud.** detekt (`config/detekt/detekt.yml`: the defaults plus `ForbiddenImport`, `ForbiddenMethodCall`, `GlobalCoroutineUsage`, `SuspendFunSwallowedCancellation`), run with type resolution per source set; every active rule is an error. `MagicNumber` is on in production code: a number the product retunes is a knob (`com.example.config`). Compiler warnings are errors (`allWarningsAsErrors`). ktfmt (kotlinlang style) owns layout; `./gradlew ktfmtFormat` fixes it, and detekt's `MaxLineLength` is off so the two can't disagree. A real exception is `@Suppress("<Rule>")` on the one declaration with a why-comment, never a config change.
- **Expected failures are values.** Return `Outcome<T, E>` (`domain/.../Outcome.kt`) or a sealed result type, with `E` a sealed interface handled by an exhaustive `when` (no `else` over your own sealed types, so a new case fails to compile where it isn't handled). Not `kotlin.Result`: its failure is any `Throwable`. Throw only for a broken invariant (`check`, `require`, `error`). A port's contract names its failures; an adapter maps vendor exceptions to them.
- **Coroutines.** Ports are `suspend` functions. Timeouts and backoff use `withTimeoutOrNull` and `delay` from kotlinx-coroutines, not a clock port: they run on the caller's dispatcher, which tests replace with virtual time. Wall-clock time is a port (`WallClock`), because it can be wrong. No `GlobalScope`; no `runCatching` around a suspend call (it swallows cancellation).
- **Parse at the boundary.** Adapters turn bytes and rows into domain types; `:domain` takes valid values.
- **Versions** live in `gradle/libs.versions.toml` only; repositories only in `settings.gradle.kts` (a module that adds one fails the build). detekt is on its 2.0 line (alpha) because 1.23 is built on Kotlin 2.0; keep `kotlin` equal to the version detekt is built on.
- **Tools.** JDK 21 (CI: Temurin), an Android SDK for `app/` (`bash stacks/kotlin/android-sdk.sh` installs the command-line tools in `~/Android/Sdk` and records the license; point `ANDROID_HOME` at it, and AGP downloads the platform and build-tools `gradle/libs.versions.toml` names on first build), and the Gradle wrapper (`./gradlew`, version pinned with a checksum in `gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.properties`; never a system Gradle). Configuration cache and build cache are on (`gradle.properties`). The edit hook compiles, detekts, and format-checks the edited file's source set (on `:app`, its debug variant's tasks; `stacks/kotlin/check-edited.mjs`, a few seconds with a warm daemon), and never reports a missing JDK as clean; `pnpm kotlin:check` names the fix when the SDK is missing.
- **Android is the composition root, nothing else.** `:app` is the one Android module (the row's `android = true`: the root build gives it the Android Gradle Plugin, with AGP's built-in Kotlin, instead of Kotlin/JVM). It constructs adapters and wires them into use-cases (`AppGraph`, built once by `NotesApplication`); keep it thin, since everything in it needs an Android build to check. Every other module is plain Kotlin/JVM, so an Android API doesn't compile in `:domain` or `:usecases` and their tests stay on the host (`pnpm kotlin:gates` plants one in each). An Android row must allow `ANY_LIBRARY` and carry no bytecode rules (the scan doesn't run on Android), and no module may depend on it. An adapter wrapping an Android API lives in `:app` for now (`docs/deferred.md` → the Android leg's open ends). No Android app? Delete `app/`, its `include` in `settings.gradle.kts`, and its `LAYERS` row, then in `.github/workflows/ci.yml` the `android-emulator` job, the **Android SDK** step in Checks, and the **Classify Android paths** step (with its `android` output); the emulator job would otherwise fail on a project with no Android module.
- **Android lint is loud too.** `check` runs lint on the debug variant with `abortOnError` and `warningsAsErrors`; `LINT_DISABLED` in `build.gradle.kts` is the one list of checks that stay off (version-staleness checks, which go red on Google's release days, not on a change here). A baseline, `abortOnError`/`warningsAsErrors` off, `ignoreWarnings`, `checkOnly`, a `lintConfig` or any `lint.xml`, and a check disabled, ignored, or made informational outside that list fail the build. A real exception is `@SuppressLint("<Check>")` or `//noinspection <Check>` on the one declaration with a `//` reason above it, or `tools:ignore="<Check>"` on the one XML element (an XML comment saying why; not checked); `@SuppressLint("all")`, `//noinspection ALL`, `tools:ignore="all"`, and `<!--suppress ALL -->` fail `checkSuppressions`, which reads every production source set (main, debug, release) and any resource directory a build file adds. A gate task turned off (`enabled = false`) or left out of `check` (`-x`, `excludedTaskNames`, `check`'s dependencies replaced) fails the build. User-facing text lives in `res/values/strings.xml`.
- Layout: `domain/` (`com.example.config` knobs, `com.example.domain`), `usecases/` (`com.example.usecases.<use-case>`: use-case + ports; fakes in `src/testFixtures`), `data/` (`com.example.data`, one adapter per vendor), `app/` (`com.example.app`: `AppGraph`, the application class, screens; `res/`; the Android `namespace` and `applicationId` in `app/build.gradle.kts`). The package `com.example` is a placeholder: rename it with the directories (detekt's `InvalidPackageDeclaration` checks they match).
